WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.215
- [Cesar] In my family,
arepas is serious business.

00:00:02.215 --> 00:00:03.665
If you ever came late to breakfast,

00:00:03.665 --> 00:00:05.589
there was a really good chance
you weren't gonna have one.

00:00:05.589 --> 00:00:08.433
Hi I'm Cesar, and today we're
making my family's arepas.

00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:09.984
I was born in Caracas, Venezuela.

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:12.465
When I was 6, we left Venezuela
for the United States.

00:00:12.465 --> 00:00:16.881
Arepas are a type of bread
that Venezuelans eat.

00:00:16.881 --> 00:00:18.893
We eat it for breakfast,
lunch, and dinner.

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:20.309
The one important thing you wanna remember

00:00:20.309 --> 00:00:23.555
is that the cornmeal has to
be poured slowly to the water

00:00:23.555 --> 00:00:25.499
to eliminate any chance of having clumps.

00:00:25.499 --> 00:00:27.349
At the end of the stirring,
you want to make sure

00:00:27.349 --> 00:00:29.370
you have a very dense dough ball.

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:31.189
You know the arepas
dough is ready when you

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:33.554
press your fingers against it
and it doesn't stick to them.

00:00:33.554 --> 00:00:35.598
The story behind my parents
is they met in college.

00:00:35.598 --> 00:00:37.099
My father was from Venezuela,

00:00:37.099 --> 00:00:38.902
and my mother was from Bristol, Virginia.

00:00:38.902 --> 00:00:41.543
He was on a scholarship
and he had an opportunity

00:00:41.543 --> 00:00:44.035
to come here and learn, but caveat to that

00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:46.403
was that he was gonna have
to return to Venezuela

00:00:46.403 --> 00:00:47.755
and spend some time working there.

00:00:47.755 --> 00:00:49.835
Which meant, after my
mom and he fell in love,

00:00:49.835 --> 00:00:51.115
she was gonna have to come with him.

00:00:51.115 --> 00:00:53.315
She had a lot of struggles
and culture shock

00:00:53.315 --> 00:00:55.211
when she was in Venezuela,
but one of the things

00:00:55.211 --> 00:00:58.379
that she really could confide
in was learning how to make

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:01.166
traditional Venezuelan
dishes with my grandmother.

00:01:01.166 --> 00:01:03.191
So the great thing about arepas is

00:01:03.191 --> 00:01:05.106
you can really have them with anything.

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:06.819
But my favorite way that it's prepared is

00:01:06.819 --> 00:01:09.277
my mother's chicken avocado recipe.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:11.674
It's something that we call reina pepiada.

00:01:11.674 --> 00:01:13.362
It consists of avocado, cilantro,

00:01:13.362 --> 00:01:14.913
salt and pepper, and chicken.

00:01:14.913 --> 00:01:16.032
You can used pulled chicken,

00:01:16.032 --> 00:01:17.261
but if you don't have time to make that,

00:01:17.261 --> 00:01:18.690
you can also use rotisserie style.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:21.097
Arepas were always a big
part of our breakfast.

00:01:21.097 --> 00:01:23.584
We used to have them with scrambled eggs,

00:01:23.584 --> 00:01:24.853
ham and cheese, and butter.

00:01:24.853 --> 00:01:26.706
One of the things that
I'm really proud about

00:01:26.706 --> 00:01:28.574
for having two parents
from different cultures,

00:01:28.574 --> 00:01:30.342
is the perspective I have on the world.

00:01:30.342 --> 00:01:31.942
I get to appreciate so many things

00:01:31.942 --> 00:01:34.270
because I've seen it from
so many different angles.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.710
I'm really thankful to my parents

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:38.327
for keeping up with our
traditions from our native land.

00:01:38.327 --> 00:01:40.802
Now I'm here at Tasty
Studios making my own video,

00:01:40.802 --> 00:01:43.348
so I really feel like it's
the torch being passed down.

00:01:43.348 --> 00:01:45.457
Oh, yes!

00:01:45.457 --> 00:01:48.624
(light music)

